WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.680
llama.cpp can often be used through prebuilt binaries or built from source at a high

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:05.456
level.

00:00:05.456 --> 00:00:08.060
Prebuilt binaries are convenient for getting started quickly.

00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:12.195
Building from source can provide more control over acceleration options, platform support,

00:00:12.195 --> 00:00:13.360
and compiler settings.

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:16.335
The right path depends on operating system, hardware, comfort level,

00:00:16.335 --> 00:00:20.425
and whether the system is a short experiment or a repeatable AI workstation.

00:00:20.425 --> 00:00:23.480
Windows, Linux, and macOS each have their own considerations.

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:26.512
CPU-only builds are usually the simplest starting point.

00:00:26.512 --> 00:00:31.269
GPU-enabled builds may use acceleration paths such as CUDA, Metal, Vulkan, ROCm,

00:00:31.269 --> 00:00:34.380
or other backend options depending on platform and hardware.

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:38.560
Those paths can improve performance, but they also introduce driver, compiler, library,

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:41.085
and version compatibility concerns.

00:00:41.085 --> 00:00:42.619
Installation should be documented and repeatable.

00:00:42.619 --> 00:00:47.005
Record the llama.cpp version or release, operating system, driver stack, build options,

00:00:47.005 --> 00:00:50.635
acceleration backend, compiler or dependency notes, model file,

00:00:50.635 --> 00:00:52.040
and working command examples.

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:55.900
This is especially important when a setup supports real workflows or when you expect to

00:00:55.900 --> 00:00:57.240
reproduce it on another machine.

00:00:57.240 --> 00:00:59.920
Avoid random version mixing without a rollback plan.

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:02.295
A runtime update, driver update, model change,

00:01:02.295 --> 00:01:04.640
or new build option can improve performance,

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:07.080
but it can also break a previously stable workflow.

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:10.615
Stable local inference comes from controlled changes, known-good configurations,

00:01:10.615 --> 00:01:13.160
and basic validation after each meaningful update.
